# London Tube Map: Blue Plaques

London’s famous blue plaques link the people of the past with the buildings of the present. Now run by English Heritage, the London blue plaques scheme was started in 1866 and is thought to be the oldest of its kind in the world.

More than 1,000 plaques across the capital, on buildings humble and grand, honour the notable women and men who have lived or worked in them.

The London Underground is London's rapid transit system serving Greater London and some parts of adjacent counties. The Underground has its origins in 1863 as the world's first underground passenger railway and has now expanded to 11 lines with 400km of track. 

With its 272 stations it accommodates up to 5 million passenger journeys a day and is a part of daily life.

XXXXX - Work this out how to combine.

## How does it work?

Using [OpenPlaques](https://openplaques.org/data#) we gather London's blue plaques, combine this with the London Tube to generate a map of London.


## Running it

The virtual environment lives at the repo root; the code lives in `src/`.

First time only:

    python3 -m venv .venv
    .venv/bin/pip install -r requirements.txt

Build the database — this downloads the latest TfL feed, versions any changes
into SQLite, and rebuilds the derived views:

    cd src && ../.venv/bin/python -m handler.main

Or, without changing directory, from the repo root:

    PYTHONPATH=src .venv/bin/python -m handler.main

Both write to `src/data/london.db`. Nothing under `src/data/` is committed to
git, so run this after cloning.

Three things have to line up, which is why the command looks the way it does:
the venv's `python` (the system one does not have the dependencies), `-m`
rather than a file path (so `src/` goes on `sys.path` instead of
`src/handler/`), and `src/` as the working directory.

## Layout

    src/
    ├── handler/main.py             entry point: runs every source, rebuilds the views, writes the map
    ├── layout_overrides.json       hand-tuning of the map, applied on top of the generated layout
    ├── role_categories.md          the themed maps on offer - edit this to add one
    ├── utils/
    │   ├── db.py                   versioned (SCD type 2) SQLite storage
    │   ├── distances.py            haversine distances between coordinates
    │   ├── layout.py               the octilinear geometry: placing, routing, validating
    │   ├── mapdata.py              assembles src/web/map.json
    │   ├── categories.py           role_categories.md -> the themed maps
    │   ├── plaque_stations.py      which station each plaque belongs to
    │   ├── sources/plaques.py      OpenPlaques London dump: find, download, load
    │   ├── sources/tfl.py          TfL station data: download, load, coordinates
    │   ├── sources/tfl_network.py  TfL route sequences: which stations each line calls at
    │   └── sql/                    derived views, reapplied on every build
    └── web/                        the map, as a static page

## The map

    cd src/web && python3 -m http.server 8000

Then open <http://localhost:8000>. The map sizes itself to fill the window on
load and on resize; drag to pan, scroll to zoom, click a station to see what the
database holds for it.

Options, top left, holds the two switches the drawing itself responds to:

  * **Overground lines** - off to start with. Hiding the six Overground lines
    takes the 83 stations only they call at with them, and drops the interchange
    rings from the two dozen tube stops whose only connection was one of them.
  * **Dark mode** - follows the machine's own setting until it is touched. The
    paper and the ink flip; TfL's line colours mostly do not, bar the few too
    dark to make out against a dark backdrop, and the Northern line's black,
    which is ink rather than a colour and flips with the page.

The page is plain HTML - no build step, no node_modules - and draws
[d3-tube-map](https://github.com/johnwalley/d3-tube-map) from a CDN. Note that
it pins d3 v6: d3-tube-map 1.5.0 will not work with v7.

`src/web/map.json` is generated, but it is committed, so the page works straight
from a clone without waiting on a build.

### Where the shape comes from

The 19 lines, 421 stations and 580 stretches of track are all live TfL data. The
*positions* are not: the real Tube map is a copyrighted design, licensed through
Pinder Creative on terms that forbid altering it - renaming the stations very
much included - so this draws its own diagram of the same network instead.

d3-tube-map will not accept an arbitrary polyline. Every segment has to run
along one of the eight compass points, and a change of direction needs its own
node placed one incoming plus one outgoing step past the end of the run. So
`utils/layout.py` earns the geometry in three passes:

  * **place** - project each station, then pull it towards the centre by the
    square root of its distance. Beck's map gives the centre most of the room
    and squeezes the ends of the lines; without that compression the grid would
    have to be about 1000 cells wide to keep Covent Garden and Leicester Square
    apart, and would be mostly empty. Stations then take an integer cell,
    busiest first, so interchanges land where they asked to.
  * **route** - join each pair of stations with straight runs and corners,
    trying short sequences before long ones and preferring the shortest drawn
    line, which is what puts the diagonals in. A line leaves a station the way
    it arrived, so the turning happens in between; four corners is a full
    reversal, which is the worst any line asks for.
  * **validate** - a port of the library's own `populateLineDirections`, run
    over every branch before the JSON is written, so a geometry mistake fails
    the build rather than the browser.
  * **label** - decide which side of each station its name sits on, and how far
    out. Each name is measured into a rectangle - a long one needs more room
    than a short one - and scored on what it would cover: the lines, and the
    names already placed. Landing on another name costs more than crossing a
    line, since two names on top of each other are unreadable where a name over
    a line is merely untidy. Crowded stations choose first, and then the worst
    few are re-placed a few times over against everything else.

The build prints how it did - `0 overlapping, 56 close to a line` - which is
worth watching when hand-tuning. The overlap count is exact; the line count is
pessimistic, treating a line as filling every cell it crosses.

### Hand-tuning it

The generated layout is geographically honest rather than beautiful, and the
place to improve it is `src/layout_overrides.json`, which is applied last and
so survives a rebuild:

    {
      "stations": { "Bank": { "coords": [104, -66], "labelPos": "E" } },
      "lines":    { "circle": { "shiftNormal": 1 } }
    }

Stations are keyed the way `map.json` keys them - `OxfordCircus`,
`KingsCrossStPancras` - and `shiftNormal` is what holds two lines apart where
they share track.

## The plaques

`utils/sources/plaques.py` finds the latest London dump linked from
[OpenPlaques](https://openplaques.org/posts/data) - 3,781 plaques, of which
3,675 have been given a position - and versions it into the `plaques` table.

`utils/plaque_stations.py` then attributes each one to the three stations
nearest it, using the haversine distances in `distances.py`: one call measures
every station against every plaque, 421 by 3,675, and the ranking is a sort down
each column. Anything more than a kilometre from a station is dropped, which is
about a twelve minute walk and keeps 3,238 of them. That fills the
`plaque_stations` table, which is computed rather than fetched and so is rebuilt
each run rather than versioned.

Three stations each rather than one, because the closest station is not always
the one a person would use, and a map wanting a *different* plaque at every
station needs somewhere to fall back to.

The `station_plaques` view joins the three together, and is the one to query:

    SELECT station_name, person, role, distance_m
    FROM station_plaques
    WHERE rank = 1 AND role = 'composer'
    ORDER BY distance_m;

`role` is the plaque's `lead_subject_primary_role`, which is filled in for 3,186
of them and runs to 1,091 different values - poet, novelist, actor, composer,
singer - which is what a map of musicians or politicians gets built from.
`roles` holds the fuller list where one is given.

The view drops plaques with nobody named or no role recorded. Plenty of what
survives still commemorates a place or a thing rather than a person - a housing
estate, a bicycle club, a tube station - and `subject_type` is what separates
them: `man` and `woman` are the people. Prefer it to `gender`, which records 95
men as `object` and cannot be trusted for that. `person_image` links a portrait
where one exists and `plaque_photo` a picture of the plaque itself.

An absent value in the dump is an empty field, which would otherwise reach
SQLite as `''` and quietly defeat every `IS NULL` test, so the loader makes
those null.

## The themed maps

The triangle at the top right opens the list: Music, Women of London, Politician
& government, and so on. Picking one relabels every station it has somebody for
and fades the rest, so what a theme covers reads at a glance.

What is on that list comes from `src/role_categories.md`, and **that file is the
only thing to edit to add a category** - no code changes. It has three levels:

    ## Arts & Literature          a group, which also becomes a map of its own
    - Music                       a category within it
      - composer                  a role, matched against the plaque's
      - subject_type: woman       or a column and value, for anything not a role

Roles are matched case-insensitively and with curly apostrophes folded to
straight ones, which is otherwise an easy way to list a role that silently
matches nothing. A `field: value` entry may use `subject_type`, `gender`,
`colour` or `area`; anything else is rejected by name rather than ignored.

Each group gets a combined map as well as its parts, because several categories
are thin on their own - Education covers five stations - but good combined.
Only people are included: `man` and `woman`, so the plaques for churches, public
houses and bomb sites stay out of it.

A station shows one name, and a person appears once. The nearest pairings are
taken first and the rest fall back to the second or third station recorded for
that plaque, which is what those were kept for - so Music fills 87 stations
where only 71 have a musician as their single nearest plaque.

The result is written to `src/web/categories.json`, separate from the map so
adding a category does not rewrite the map itself, and fetched only when the
menu is first opened.

## The database

`src/data/london.db` keeps the full history of every row. A row version records
the load it first appeared in (`valid_from_load`) and the load that replaced or
removed it (`valid_to_load`), so re-running the build against an unchanged feed
writes nothing at all. Each table has a `current_<table>` view showing only the
live rows, and `loads` records every ingestion run.

Two sources feed it. `tfl_stationdata` is the zip of CSVs TfL publish, which is
where station names and coordinates come from; the extracted CSVs are deleted
once they are in the database. `tfl_network` is the Unified API, which is where
the lines and their running order come from. Unregistered API callers get 50
requests a minute and a build makes about 40, so two builds in quick succession
will pause and retry; setting `TFL_APP_KEY` avoids the wait.

The views in `utils/sql/` join the two together. The wrinkle they handle is that
the API calls Euston `940GZZLUEUS` while the station data files it under its hub
code `HUBEUS`; falling back to `topMostParentId` is what gets all 469 stops on
all 19 lines onto a row with coordinates.
